n cancer  ultimately relapse and then  treatment goes on from there um and  after several treatments typically uh  death ensues in these patients  so our current uh strategies for  treating ovarian cancer are not that  effective in in terms of preventing  death although they can prolong the life  of the individual so there's a continued  need to  develop new therapies that can fight  ovarian cancer and so certainly one of  the most important areas that is being  evaluated in the field of oncology today  is the development of immune-based  therapies  like other cancers in ovarian cancer  we've known now for  several decades that  the immune system has a role in  the natural outcome of disease this is a  study that was published in  2003 from dr  zhang and and kenejo garcia with dr  kukos at the university of pennsylvania  where they analyzed  several hundred  specimens from ovarian cancer patients  for the infiltration of immune effectors  specifically focusing on t cells finding  that those individuals that had uh  evidence of t cell infiltration into  their tumor had improved  disease-free survival and so i'm not  going to go into  an extensive  overview of what the  immune system is but just to point out  that there are two arms to the immune  system the innate and the adaptive  immune arm  the adaptive immune arm is where uh t  cells come from and where b cells and  antibodies come from and beyond that  there is it's also known that the immune  system can differentiate depending on  the type of insult whether it's a you  know cancer  or a virus or bacteria or something like  that i like to think of it as there's  multiple flavors of the immune response  and some of these are important in  eradicating cancer and some of those  have a pathologic role in cancer so for  example regulatory t cells  are a component of the adaptive immune  response  and are known to regulate the immune  response to  an antigenic insult by suppressing or  limiting the clonal expansion of the  immune response and also play a role in  protecting  ovarian cancers as well as a wide  variety of other cancers from the immune  response in fact in ovarian cancer there  are a number of different mechanisms  which have been identified by our group  and other groups throughout the last  couple of decades  that clearly demonstrate that  the ovarian cancers can  attract in a number of different cell  types that can block an effective  anti-tumor  immune response i had already mentioned  that  regulatory t cells were a main component  of that immune response in fact this is  a diagram here  showing  the  staining of a tissue section for  regulatory t cells the combination of  cd4 cd25 and fox p3 and as you can see  here you can see these  little cells that  are stained blue in the nucleus those  are the regulatory t cells they  constitute about one third of the total  infiltrating cd4 population the cd4  population  being the dominant  t cell infil infiltrate in ovarian  cancer so with that said  ovarian cancer is  is  targeted by the immune system but also  it induces local tolerance that prevents  the immune system uh from attacking it  as a result of that our strategy has  been to develop  strategies to boost host immune defenses  to prevent disease  following  standard conventional therapies like  surgery and chemotherapy so there's  windows of opportunity for many cancers  as well as ovarian cancer in which  following a standard surgery and  chemotherapy that one could think about  using strategies to boost the immune  response in our case we've been  interested in the development of  vaccines to boost  t cells in antibody responses  hopefully to ultimately prevent  recurrence and it seems to be an  approach that  will ultimately be found to be  successful so when we think about the  development of  vaccines we think about well what are we  going to target and  how are we going to formulate that into  a vaccine so there are a number of  different approaches that people are  using to develop cancer vaccines one  certainly is the targeting of microbial  derived antigens that may be important  in cancers such as head and neck cancer  or cervical cancer but there's no  microbial etiologic agents associated  with ovarian cancer so we have to turn  to  other antigens one  source of antigens could be mutated  mutation derived neoantigens which are  present one of the problems with  targeting neoantigens or  neoantigens that are derived from  mutations is that they're personalized  for each individual patient  as  mutation derived neoantigens are often  found to be  only in a you know single tumor or  rarely in a few individuals  and most of them are are personal  we've been interested in in developing  vaccines based on over expressed cell  proteins as a source of what we call  tumor subdominant neoantigens and this  is based on work that goes back into  the  1980s and 1990s from  several pioneering  immunologists that  uh demonstrated long ago that your body  develops tolerance against subdominate  excuse me against dominant epitopes that  are presented on  self mhc either class 1 or class 2  molecules  and importantly in cancers over  expression can lead to  what we call or what we term  subdominant neo antigens and that's  shown in this figure here where dominant  antigens are shown in blue and  subdominant antigens are shown in red so  as overexpression occurs aberrant  processing aberrant tumor  cell localization occurs and that  results in the presentation of unusual  epitopes derived from other parts of the  protein  that is over  expressed so using a variety of  different approaches  including bioinformatics  mhc binding and t-cell activation assays  as well as the assessment of natural  pre-existent immunity  in individuals we can formulate vaccines  and we've been interested in several  different antigens and one in particular  for ovarian cancer and antigen folate  receptor alpha is over expressed in  about 95 percent of cases  that we see stage  typically stage  three and stage four high grade serous  ovarian cancer and to formulate a  vaccine we use bioinformatics and we  determine  using bioinformatics the  epitopes that would be bound to mhc  class 2 molecules so we're very much  interested as opposed to  identifying cd8 t-cell inducing vaccines  cd4  t-cell-inducing vaccines due to the  central role of cd4s in uh developing  immune responses  they're as as many of you know  involved in in orchestrating the  development of antibodies orchestrating  the development of macrophage driven or  nk cell driven immune responses as well  as cd8 t cell responses so our focus has  been on identifying mhc class 2 or hla  class ii binding epitopes that we can  formulate and particularly interested in  identifying those in which an existing t  cell repertoire is available so in this  rather simplified overview of that  strategy we use bioinformatics and we  predict epitopes along the continuum of  folate receptor alpha and then we  identify which of those epitopes  actually binds to mhc class ii molecules  and then we select out of those which  bind to multiple mhc class 2 molecules  that we would see in the population  that's at risk for recurrence of ovarian  cancer and then conduct t cell  activation assays and verify using  natural pre-existent immunity type  assays  to determine  those epitopes that could be formulated  in the vaccine so in this particular  case we identified several different  epitopes and the ones shown in red that  are the ones that we ultimately selected  as being  potential subdominant neo antigens that  are expressed as a result of  overexpression of folate receptor in the  tumor microenvironment as well as in the  regional lymph nodes and we formulated  this vaccine and compounded it with  gmcsf and adjuvant and initially  delivered it now going on about 10 years  ago  to patients with breast and ovarian  cancer to demonstrate number one in a  phase one clinical trial that we could  specifically target these subdominant  neo antigens  in a vaccine  safely in individuals and generate  immune responses so the upper graph  shows the development of immunity pre  and post to the vaccine  components fr 30 56  76 113 and 238 following immunization  over the course of six months and we see  that we could  also detect immunity to these  epitopes in follow-up  anywhere between 12 and 18 months in  individuals and while i don't have  the slide here that shows the safety  analysis in general immunizing in this  fashion using subdominant neoantigens is  a  very safe approach and typically the  only adverse events that we see  associated with vaccination  include what you would see with other  vaccines  such as skin site  reactions or general fatigue  and possibly even fever  so with that said uh after we conducted  that clinical trial we became interested  in the study that was published by  weiping zoo from the university of  michigan and followed on by a couple of  other investigative groups  that had demonstrated that il-17 was  associated with improved survival in  ovarian cancer and that's shown on the  left-hand slide here individuals with  high levels of il-17 in their tumor  micro environment as opposed to low  tend to have much better survival  recurrence-free survival than those  individuals that had lower levels and  what's important is that we saw also  that  or the data showed also that individuals  with high levels of th17 tended to have  much reduced levels of regulatory t  cells thus they had less immune  suppressive potential in their tumor  microenvironment so if we could develop  vaccines that not only targeted  subdominant epitopes  but also generated  il-17 or th17 responses  we hypothesized that we could  potentially protect individuals from  relapse following conventional therapies  but to do that we had to figure out a  solution for being able to specifically  induce the  th17 t-cell subset following vaccination  so at that time  back in the early 2010s we  came across  dr martin cannon's studies in which he  was looking at the modulation of p-38  map kinase and dendritic cells  along with il-15 co-stimulation  finding that if you use that approach  strictly in in vitro assays he could  induce il-17 producing t cells and thus  we thought there was an opportunity for  us to incorporate the recipe  that he developed into a clinical recipe  for  developing dendritic cells that induced  th17 t cells and ultimately following  pulsing of those dendritic cells to  induce th17 tail  th17t cells that were specific  for  the folate receptor alpha  and therefore we came up with a  cocktail that we moved clinically this  is data  actually showing uh from uh preclinical  mouse studies in which il-15  co-stimulation of np-38  map kinase inhibition  suppressed il-12 and il-10 and that  favored the generation of th17 i'm not  going to go into all of the data  but how it did that was  by suppressing the il-12 driven th1  t-cell component and allowing the  development of th17  due to the fact that it suppressed those  specific uh cytokines such as style 12  while at the same time  maintaining importance cytokines such as  il-6 il-1 alpha or excuse me il-1 beta  and tgf beta which are important to the  development of th17  t-cell effectors so then we designed a  clinical trial  and worked with  the mayo clinic  ovarian cancer spore as well as  benefactors to come up with a  funding for a phase one clinical trial  in 19 patients with the primary goal  of looking at safety and immunogenicity  of a special th17 inducing dendritic  cell vaccine that can induce th17 t  cells specific for  the folate receptor alpha and that  clinical trial um is now completed and  was published in in nature  communications in 2020 uh just briefly  an overview individuals  were given a leukopheresis at  registration and we produced dendritic  cells pulsed with the folate receptor  peptides and ultimately immunize those  individuals five  times  every three weeks followed by boosters  periodically and then followed those  individuals up for  a couple of years  following the initiation of vaccines so  i'm going to touch on just some of that  data  demonstrating that we were able to  accomplish the goal of inducing th17  t cell effectors in these individuals  and this is shown here kind of in a  nutshell  looking at the mean levels of antigen  specific not only th17 but also th1  cells specific for each of the epitopes  that were in the vaccine as well as the  response to the native folate receptor  alpha including controls tetanus toxin  and cyclin d1 as you can appreciate on  the left side  we saw  an escalation in the  levels of th1t cells that's something  that we didn't expect  quite as high as we saw in the previous  clinical trials but importantly we saw  the ability of the vaccine to induce  th17  t cells as well so  we we achieved our primary goal of being  able to induce th17  and  we also found due to the  rather degenerate nature of the epitopes  that we use that we were able to  generate immune responses th1 and th17  in the vast majority  of individuals  and interestingly enough and something  that we didn't see with just peptide  vaccine alone is that about half of the  patients responded to the dendritic cell  vaccine with peptide specific antibodies  so you would think that if we pulse  these  epitopes on dendritic cells that we  would generate predominantly uh t you  know t cell responses but that wasn't  the case we saw  for at least uh uh three or four of the  peptides uh  good evidence of the generation of  antibody responses uh in fact the  generation of these antibody responses  led to about 50 percent of individuals  developing antibodies that could  recognize  a folate receptor expression  at the cell surface and when we did an  analysis looking at the bottom right  hand graphs to see if those peptides  were or excuse me those antibodies were  specific for peptides we found indeed  that they were so antibodies were being  generated  against the epitopes that were being  presented by the  dendritic cells interestingly enough but  most importantly out of all of this we  had the opportunity to follow up the  patients  for a couple of years after the  beginning of vaccination as i had  mentioned  and this data is uh was was relatively  shocking to us that while we would have  expected you know perhaps 85 percent of  patients to have relapsed in 24  months following enrollment on study we  in fact found that about 40 percent of  the patients uh appeared to have been  cured going out to about 50 or 60 months  in fact that still holds  even you know many months after the  initial publication and like we found  with our  previous peptide vaccine  there were minimal adverse events  injection site reactions were the most  common adverse event there was some pain  associated with uh vaccination after the  vaccinations were given but in general  the  uh adverse events were typically grade  one and grade two and were very uh  transient uh in nature  immunity was also associated with uh  recurrence when i looked at those  individuals that had no recurrence  versus those that did have recurrence we  could see that both the th1 immune  response and the antibody response but  interestingly not the il-17 response  were associated with improved survival  indicating that there was some  interactions or some coordination  amongst all of these immune effectors in  the tumor microenvironment to eradicate  disease and also importantly we found  that immunity was persistent so looking  at those patients that didn't recur long  uh  after a long time after the ending of  vaccine  at two years  you could see that they still maintained  high levels of th1t cells il17 or th17 t  cells as well as antibodies so a very  impressive outcome uh with this  particular vaccine and we also observed  that the antibodies  were  [Music]  able to induce antibody dependent  cellular cytotoxicity which was also  associated with recurrence-free  survival  as well so it looked to us that there  was a  coordinated immune response that  involved many different types of immune  effectors that were generated with this  vaccine that could possibly be  contributing to  the destruction  of the tumor and we also looked in those  individuals that had disease recurrence  and found despite the fact that they had  recurrence that there was some  important changes now on one hand we  didn't see any evidence of immunoediting  so when we generated immunity against  the folate receptor alpha we had thought  you know perhaps that those patients  that did recur would uh  recurs with uh  antigen loss variants we didn't see any  evidence of that in the first  left-hand slide or excuse me graph in  which we  plotted out the flight receptor  expression based on h score we didn't  see any evidence of infiltration of cd8  t cells into the tumor microenvironment  so cda t cells had been previously found  to be associated with disease recurrence  so this is interesting because in our  mouse studies which i'll show on the  next slide we saw no evidence of any  involvement of cd8 t cells in fact the  the  vaccine  seems to be protective and and dependent  exclusively on cd4 t cells whereas what  we did see is that the induction of of  th17 in those patients that did receive  vaccine and recurred the  down regulation of regulatory t cells as  we would have expected based on this  reciprocal a relationship that was  already observed in prior studies with  th17 and regulatory t cells and those  tumors that  did relapse following vaccination all  had high levels of pdl-1  many of them had very low levels prior  to the administration of vaccine  indicating that there was an active  immune response going on in the tumor  microenvironment and this is the data  from the mirroring model in which we  recapitulate the  survival that we see in individuals  shown in the upper  left-hand  graph is a comparison between  antigen-pulsed conventional dendritic  cells and antigen pulse th17 dc shown in  the blue  bar which demonstrates that this type of  vaccine approaches is superior to that  than conventional disease but most  importantly in the middle  graph on the top there is a  demonstration that the inclusion of  anti-cd4 which depletes cd4 t cells  during the course of immunization  completely reversed the protective  effect of the vaccine whereas anti-cd8  had no impact and specifically we only  were able to detect il-17 in the  peritoneal cavities of mice with  ovarian cancer with the th17 vaccine  demonstrating the specific induction  with this approach of il-15  co-stimulation and p-38 map kinase  inhibition and importantly also those  tumors  that did relapse in these or that did  recur in these individuals were  infiltrated with higher levels of cd4 t  cells as opposed to cda t cells  demonstrating to us that the primary  adaptive immune mechanisms that were  generated following the vaccination  where cd8 t cells excuse me cd4 t cell  responses as well as antibody responses  and probably a coordinated effort among  different subsets of t cells as well as  with antibodies and ultimately we think  myeloid effectors in the tumor  microenvironment such as macrophages  played a role in eradication of the  tumor based on our mouse modeling  experiments  which i do not have time to present  today  and based on all of this we  are moving forward with a phase two uh  trial um mc1963  at the mayo clinic in which we will test  this vaccine  in a randomized placebo-controlled  clinical trial that aims to enroll a  total of 78 patients two-thirds of those  patients will be randomized to arm one  and we'll see receive the vaccine  in much the same manner that they  received in the phase one clinical trial  with the ultimate goal of determining  whether or not as we would suspect that  it prevents relapse in individuals and  so that trial is set to be activated  within the next year it's part of the  mayo clinic ovarian cancer spore  specific aim one is to look at  progression-free survival  specific aim2 to  understand the types of immune responses  and give a better handle on why patients  are in some cases resistant  to the vaccine-induced  t cell and antibody responses and then  follow that on with more mechanistic  studies in mouse models to help us try  to understand  better  what is  the primary mechanisms by which the  tumor is eradicated following  immunization  there are also opportunities to  potentially give this in combination  with immune checkpoint blockade we found  in our prior studies that aisle 10  is  released  in the tumor microenvironment at very  high levels following  the  treatment with  isle  anti-pd1 monotherapy  and il-10 suppresses the generation of  immune responses making  these particular tumors resistant to  anti-pd1 therapy and what we found with  vaccine in the far right hand graph is  that  the vaccination prevented the ielts the  anti-pd1 induced aisle  10 release into the tumor micro  environment and collaborated with the  vaccine to provide  superior protection in in these mice  that have been challenged with ovarian  cancer ongoing research continues but  what we think is happening is a complex  set of events that involves as i had  mentioned exclusively cd4 t cells  and antibodies along with  macrophages and other myeloid cells that  migrate into the tumor microenvironment  one dominant myeloid cell is the  eosinophils so th17 we think coordinates  the activity through elaboration of a  wide variety of different  substances that  would support the generation of  macrophages and eosinophils as well as  amplified the effects of  th2 immune effectors which we've  observed in the mouse models as well as  th1 effectors leading to a coordinated  anti-tumor immune response so in  conclusion we think that  in  ovarian cancer there are opportunities  windows of opportunity to boost  host immune defenses following  conventional treatment with  surgery and chemotherapy  to  boost um t cells and antibodies that  could ultimately protect individuals  from uh recurring and increased survival  certainly the survival data uh from our  uh trial that we just published in uh  nature communications that i presented  today would indicate that at least in in  a subset of individuals we are having an  impact in preventing disease rev  recurrence possibly even curing these  patients  many of them are are now outside of  standard monitoring for ovarian cancer  recurrence  and  demonstrating  good health and a strong  anti-tumor immune response  th17 immunity in the tumor  immune micro environment dissociates  as i had previously mentioned with  better outcome in patients with ovarian  cancer and that has represented an  opportunity for us to try to think of  ways to improve that  with vaccines there are other potential  opportunities for example car t cell  therapy  may be an attractive approach to  generating that as well vaccines can be  developed  that target  aberrantly expressed proteins and folate  receptor alpha is a great example in  ovarian cancer that's highly  overexpressed  and plays a role in the biologic  activity and aggressiveness of ovarian  cancer and is potentially targeted due  to its aberrant expression and cell  localization that ultimately results in  the presentation of novel subdominant  neo antigens to which the immune system  has never responded or even seen before  to which we can generate immunity and  what we know from our studies is that  th17 tsa or vaccines can induce durable  poly functional immune responses in ned  patients despite the fact that these  patients have been treated for their  cancer with aggressive chemotherapies  they can still induce these immune  responses that appear to be helpful in  prolonging survival and as i had  mentioned our future directions are to  continue with our randomized phase 2  clinical trial to  support the  potential  licensing of this applica  this particular vaccine application in  patients with ovarian cancer following  conventional therapy  but also  an interest in in using it to  test in combination with immune  checkpoint blockade which hasn't been  very effective in patients with ovarian  cancer and other cancers as well  and our future directions will also  focus on mechanisms of therapeutic  activity and resistance and we're  particularly interested in why those  individuals that did not  that responded the vaccine but did not  show any evidence of protection  following vaccination  were indeed resistant and if we can  identify those mechanisms perhaps we can  you know come up with ways that we can  tweak the vaccine  to support better outcomes in those  patients that are resistant we're  looking at a number of different  mechanisms so i'll conclude now  with  acknowledgements  of uh  this  work wasn't possible  without the input from many many people  at the mayo clinic and other  institutions as well and a special  thanks to  dr kim calley dr matt block  martin cannon from the university of  arkansas and the individuals that help  produce the gmp great dendritic cells dr  alan dies and michael gustafson as well  as a number of other physicians in the  environment at mayo clinic that  helped enroll the patients in this  clinical trial and grant support from  many individuals particularly  the initiating grant support from the  schultz family foundation  uh who provided  funding for the initial clinical trial  to get this into uh the first cohort of  individuals as well as a company tap  immune now marker therapeutics which i  commented on  at the beginning of the talk as  licensing the  epitope technology from us as well as  a variety of different grants from the  national cancer institute  as well as private foundations such as  mocha and the anderson foundation  and with that i would like to again uh  thank you uh for  participating in the meeting and  listening to our  presentation of our study thank you very  much

